## Statuses

Configured statuses:
{{range .Statuses}}
- **{{.Name}}**{{if .Description}}: {{.Description}}{{end}}
{{- end}}

The statuses split into two groups, and the split is **workflow position vs close reason** — they partition the declared statuses, so every nib carrying one of them is in exactly one group (a hand-edited nib with no `status:` is in neither: the open default keeps it, but `-s open` and `-s closed` both drop it):
- **open** = {{join .OpenStatuses ", "}} — where the work sits while it is still on the board.
- **closed** = {{join .ClosedStatuses ", "}} — *why* it left the board; each one's reason is its description above.

**`deferred` is a close reason**, not a waiting room: a good idea at the wrong time, closed but kept as a seed rather than a dead end. Compare with **draft**, which is open — a draft nib is still on the board, it just needs refinement before it can be worked. `deferred` is a status, not a priority.
{{if and .HoldingStatuses .ReleasingStatuses}}
**A {{codes .HoldingStatuses "/"}} blocker still blocks its dependents.** This is the one place the two ideas come apart: closing a blocker as {{codes .ReleasingStatuses " or "}} settles the dependency and frees everything it was gating, but closing it as {{codes .HoldingStatuses " or "}} does not — the work is coming back, so the dependency is still unmet, and a nib blocked only by such a blocker stays out of `nibs list --ready`. That blocker is itself closed, so the open default hides it: use `nibs rel <id> --rel blocked-by --all` to see every blocker that still blocks. The relation only ever returns unreleased blockers, so no status flag surfaces a {{codes .ReleasingStatuses " or "}} one — read the raw list with `nibs get <id> -f "blocked-by(id,status)"`.
{{end}}
Archiving is **not** a status. `nibs rm <id>` moves the file to `.nibs/archive/`; the nib keeps its status and stays visible in every query.

**Status filtering (`nibs list` / `nibs rel`) is open by default.** With no status flag both commands list only **open** nibs (the closed statuses hidden), and the header discloses how many were hidden — `# 12 nibs (3 hidden: {{join .ClosedStatuses "/"}} — --all to include)`. `-s`/`--status` and `--no-status` accept the two group names, `open` and `closed`, anywhere a concrete status is accepted.

An explicit `-s` overrides the open default (`-s closed` shows only the closed ones); `--all` includes every status; `--open` is shorthand for `-s open`. So **open work under a parent needs no post-filtering** — `nibs rel <id> --rel descendants -t bug` already returns only open bugs; don't hand-roll a `--json | python` status filter. `-c`/`-q` honor the open default too; add `--all` for true totals. (A filter that admits no status, e.g. `--no-status open`, is a validation error rather than a silent empty result.)

