## Body section conventions

Each nib type has standard sections (pre-filled in the TUI):
- **task**: `## Description`, `## Verification` (checklist)
- **bug**: `## Steps to Reproduce`, `## Expected vs Actual`, `## Root Cause`
- **epic**: `## Objective`, `## Acceptance Criteria` (checklist), `## Scope Boundaries`
- **milestone**: `## Goal`, `## Current Focus`, `## Key Decisions`
- **research**: `## Question`, `## Findings`, `## Decision`, `## Follow-ups`
- **feature**: no standard template (features vary too widely)

The closing record is not one of these: `nibs close` writes the summary into a `## Summary` section itself, for every close reason, adding an entry rather than replacing what is already there.

## Editing body content

`nibs body <id>` edits the body. Content always comes from the input channel (`-`/`@FILE`), never inline. Pick one operation:
```bash
nibs body <id> --set -                                  # replace the whole body from stdin
nibs body <id> --section "## Notes" --set @notes.md     # replace only the content under a heading
nibs body <id> --append -                               # append a block from stdin
nibs body <id> --replace-old "- [ ] Task" --replace-new "- [x] Task"   # replace exactly one occurrence
```
`--section` matches an existing heading at the level you SPELL: `### H` matches only a level-3 heading (never a level-2 `## H`), while a bare `H` (no `#`) matches a heading at any level. With `--create`, a missing heading is created at the spelled level (a bare heading defaults to `##`).

`--replace-old`/`--replace-new` must match exactly once: zero matches → `TEXT_NOT_FOUND`, more than one → `TEXT_AMBIGUOUS` (both exit 2, with an occurrence count). For several replacements in one atomic write, use `nibs graphql` with `bodyMod.replace` (see GraphQL).

## Reordering

`nibs mv` moves a nib among its siblings or reparents it:
```bash
nibs mv <id> --after <sibling>          # reposition after a sibling (also --before, --first)
nibs mv <id> --parent <new-parent>      # reparent (appends; add --first to reparent to front)
nibs mv <id1> <id2> <id3> --after <anchor>          # block move: slide a contiguous run of siblings
nibs mv --children-of <parent-id> <c1> <c2> <c3>    # declaratively set the full child order (all children, once)
```
Use `--children-of ""` for root-level siblings. Position a NEW nib at creation with `nibs new … --after/--before/--first`.

## Closing

`nibs close` takes a nib off the board: it checks children are done (error unless `--force`), adds the summary to the nib's `## Summary` as a dated, reason-stamped entry, merges any `## Key Decisions` into the parent, and sets a closed status.
```bash
nibs close <id> --summary -                 # {{.DefaultCloseStatus}} — summary via stdin (or --summary @file.md)
nibs close <id> --as scrapped --summary -   # a different close reason: {{join .ClosedStatuses ", "}}
nibs close <id> --summary - --force         # close even if children are incomplete
nibs close <id> --summary - --json          # machine-readable result
```
`--as` takes a **closed status** — the word it names is the word that lands in the nib's `status:`, so there is no second close-reason vocabulary. An open status is a validation error naming the closed ones. Omit `--as` and you get `{{.DefaultCloseStatus}}`.

**`## Summary` accrues.** Each close adds an entry stamped with its reason and the date, so under the `## Summary` heading of a nib closed twice you find both records:
```markdown
**Deferred 2026-07-27** — waiting on the upstream provider release

**Scrapped 2026-08-02** — superseded by nibs-abcd
```
**Closing an already-closed nib is allowed** — that is how a close reason is revised: the new entry is added beside the old one instead of overwriting it, so the first rationale survives the second close. What a revision does *not* do is retract what an earlier close wrote to the parent — the `Completed <id>: <summary>` line an earlier `--as {{.CompletionCloseStatus}}` close put in the parent's `## Current Focus` (see below) is left standing when the reason is later revised to a different one, so the parent still reads as though the work landed. When that matters, rewrite it yourself: `nibs body <parent-id> --section "## Current Focus" --set -`. Re-closing under the *same* reason is allowed too: it is how a rationale gets updated.

**What reaches the parent depends on the reason.** `## Key Decisions` merge upward for **every** close reason — why work was set aside is exactly what a later reader looks for in the parent. Only `--as {{.CompletionCloseStatus}}` rewrites the parent's `## Current Focus`: that section says what the latest progress is, and setting work aside or abandoning it is not progress, so overwriting it would erase the record of the last real progress and leave the parent reading as though nothing were happening.

**`nibs set <id> -s <a closed status>` is refused**, and the error names the `close` line to run instead. `set` owns the open statuses; `close` owns the move to a closed one. The reason is the summary: `close` requires one and `set` does not, so allowing both would make the route that records nothing the shorter one. Two boundaries worth knowing:
- It is a rule about the `set` verb, not about the data. The web UI and the TUI go through GraphQL and can still set any status directly, as can `nibs graphql` and `nibs new -s <a closed status>` (which creates a nib already closed). What the refusal closes is the shortcut that skips the summary on an existing nib.
- Only the move *into* a closed status is refused. There is no `reopen` verb — `nibs set <id> -s todo` on a closed nib is how work comes back, and it keeps working.

## Concurrency control

Optimistic locking via etags:
```bash
ETAG=$(nibs get <id> -f etag --json | jq -r '.nib.etag')
nibs set <id> --if-match "$ETAG" -s in-progress
```
On conflict the command exits 4 (`CONFLICT`) and reports the current etag. For bulk `nibs mv` (`--children-of` or block move) use `--child-if-match <id>=<etag>` (repeatable); pre-validation is atomic.

## GraphQL (the escape hatch)

`nibs graphql` runs arbitrary GraphQL for the long tail the verbs don't cover — fetch exactly the fields you need across many nibs, traverse relationships in one query, or batch mutations.
```bash
nibs graphql --json '{ nibs(filter: { excludeStatus: ["completed","scrapped"], isBlocked: false }) { id title status } }'
nibs graphql --json '{ nib(id: "tnib-abc") { title parent { title } children { id status } } }'
```
Atomic multi-edit body update (several replacements + append under one etag):
```bash
nibs graphql 'mutation {
  updateNib(id: "<id>", input: { status: "completed", bodyMod: {
    replace: [ { old: "- [ ] Task 1", new: "- [x] Task 1" }, { old: "- [ ] Task 2", new: "- [x] Task 2" } ]
    append: "## Summary\n\nAll tasks completed!"
  } }) { id etag }
}'
```
A failing query exits like the direct command that raises the same failure (a bad filter target exits 3, a stale `ifMatch` exits 4, an illegal parent exits 2 with `allowedParentTypes` when one refusal speaks for the whole response). A batch whose failures agree on a code keeps that code; failures that differ in kind but share an exit status keep the exit, reported under that class's general code (`HIERARCHY` beside a plain validation error reports `VALIDATION_ERROR`, exit 2). A batch whose failures span *different* exit classes supports no single claim, so it reports `UNCATEGORIZED` and exits 1 — every message is still on the wire; read them rather than branching on the code.

A batch mutation is **not atomic**: root mutation fields run in document order, execution does not stop at the first failure, and each one writes on its own. So a failed batch can still have committed real edits. After the refusal the message reports how the root fields ended — `graphql: nib not found; d1, d3 succeeded; d2 failed` means `d1` and `d3` completed without raising an error while `d2` raised the refusal. Never resend a `succeeded` alias. But `succeeded` is a **lower bound** on what landed, not a full accounting, and `failed` does not mean nothing was written: an error anywhere inside a field's own selection marks that whole field failed even when its write committed, and a failed `updateNib` persists its blocking targets before it ever reaches its own `ifMatch` guard. When neither list appears, the message claims nothing either way. Re-read any nib the batch named instead of trusting an etag you cached before it.

`nibs graphql --schema` prints the full SDL (also `nibs catalog schema`). `nibs graphql --help` for input syntax.
